              "text": "The iconic episode of *Only Fools and Horses* where Del Boy falls through the bar is titled **\"Yuppy Love\"**. It is **Season 6, Episode 1**, which originally aired on **January 8, 1989**. \n\nIt is widely considered one of the funniest and most famous moments in British comedy history.\n\n### How the Scene Unfolds\n* **The Setup:** Having watched the movie *Wall Street* multiple times, Del Boy (David Jason) has fully embraced the 1980s \"yuppie\" lifestyle. He trades his classic sheepskin coat for a smart trenchcoat and a red pair of braces. He and Trigger (Roger Lloyd-Pack) skip their usual haunt, The Nag's Head, to hang out at a trendy, upscale wine bar.\n* **The Setup of the Fall:** Del spots two women across the bar and starts giving Trigger a lecture on how to \"woo\" modern women. To show Trigger how it's done, Del says: *\"Play it cool, Trig, look casual. Watch this...\"*.\n* **The Fall:** Unbeknownst to Del, a bartender has just lifted up the hinged bar flap to step out from behind the counter. When Del tries to lean back smoothly and confidently against the bar, he leans on empty air and tumbles straight backward through the hatch, completely disappearing onto the floor.\n* **The Punchline:** Trigger, who is looking in the other direction, has absolutely no idea what just happened. He turns back, notices Del is gone, and begins looking around the room in absolute bewilderment. When Del finally climbs back up, he tries to brush it off and mutters to Trigger: *\"You\u2019re cramping my style!\"*.\n\n### Trivia & Fun Facts\n* **Trigger wasn't originally in the scene:** A rehearsal script discovered years later revealed that the scene was originally written without Trigger. Roger Lloyd-Pack happened to show up on set that day, so writer John Sullivan quickly decided to write him into the scene. Trigger's oblivious reaction became one of the funniest parts of the gag.\n* **A major milestone for Rodney:** \"Yuppy Love\" is also highly significant because it is the episode where Rodney meets **Cassandra** (Gwyneth Strong) for the first time.\n* **Longer episodes:** This was the very first regular *Only Fools and Horses* episode to be extended to 50 minutes (up from the traditional 30 minutes), which allowed for longer, more elaborate comedy setups.",
